requirements:
Implement in Rust as a SINGLE source file that compiles with `rustc --edition 2024`, std only. Output ONLY the Rust source code inside ```rust fences.

All listed types, functions, and struct fields must be public. All structs/enums derive Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq. Names are nonempty, case-sensitive opaque strings, compared using Rust String ordering. Input package names are unique; do not validate this precondition. Dependency lists may be unsorted and repeated. Do not mutate the inputs.

Exact public API:
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Package { pub name: String, pub dependencies: Vec<String> }
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ResolveError {
    MissingDependency { package: String, dependency: String },
    Cycle(Vec<String>),
}
pub fn install_order(packages: &[Package]) -> Result<Vec<String>, ResolveError>;

All catalog packages are relevant. Missing targets and cycles are permitted and must produce errors. Before cycle detection, inspect all dependency edges. If any targets are absent, return MissingDependency for the smallest (source package name, missing dependency name) pair, comparing source first. Thus a missing target anywhere takes precedence over any cycle. Otherwise: Detect cycles using DFS over the relevant graph: start unvisited nodes in ascending package-name order, visit each node's distinct dependency targets in ascending order, and stop at the first edge to a node on the current recursion stack. Extract that stack segment from the target through the current node; these edges point from dependent to dependency. Rotate this sequence so its lexicographically smallest name is first, preserve its direction, and append the first name again. Return Cycle with precisely this closed path. A self-edge yields [name, name]. Finish this cycle check before constructing the installation order.
If no error: return Ok(order). Return an installation order containing each relevant package exactly once, dependencies before their dependents. Repeated edges count once for ordering. At EVERY step choose the lexicographically smallest package among all currently ready packages (including newly ready packages); this is not a DFS postorder or a batched layer order. Empty relevant graphs return an empty vector.
